A||N||13||nette||Building Up an 87 2.5L Tech-4||||1||
Z||000000||nette||04-21-2001||10:05 PM||||I want to begin the early idea-gathering and planning stages of my 4cyl performance rebuild.  I would love any and all info, tips, resources, etc. for pulling this off.  I have thought alot about upgrading to a v6 or even a v8.  However, after seeing the v6 a fiero friend shoe-horned into his car I am not sure that's what I want to do.  I really like the fact the 4cyl doesn't take up so much room that you can't work on it. I will either rebuild the motor that is in my car now, or buy another 87 and build that (I love driving my fiero and I don't want to wait while the engine is rebuilt).   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>nette<BR>87 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000001||baldlobo||04-21-2001||10:31 PM||||let's see if I can help with the web sites: <A HREF="http://members.home.net/toniwryan/fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.home.net/toniwryan/fiero.htm</A>  <A HREF="http://fiero2m4.homestead.com/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero2m4.homestead.com/index.html</A>  <A HREF="http://www.amotion.com/p4.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.amotion.com/p4.html</A>  <A HREF="http://www.compcams.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.compcams.com/</A>  <A HREF="http://www.holley.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.holley.com/</A>  <A HREF="http://www.gtsonline.com/krp/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gtsonline.com/krp/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||04-21-2001||11:24 PM||||keep in mind the 87+ L4 is Very diferant from ealier years. Many things that are available for older versions aren't going to work in 87+.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||qwikgta||04-21-2001||11:35 PM||||If its room and ease you want, upgrade to a QUAD 4.  You will double your HP/TQ and still have lots of room.  <P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html||||reg||10
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||04-22-2001||12:27 AM||||This is true that the 87 with the DIS ignition and different TBI made it even more difficult to attache most of the Holley upgrades made for the 84 to 86 Dukes. The bigger TBI could work but there would be an awful lot of rerouting of cables and fuel lines.<P>I had an 87 coupe and I loved the heck out of it when I had it. I tinkered with it in a number of ways to the point where I could almost keep up with the V6s in my club but still have something resembling gas mileage!<P>And honestly, the V6 doesn't take up that much room. If you were to take off the trunk lid and look down at it you would see how easily the V8s fit and how small the V6 really looks in that engine bay!<P>I keep hearing about the Quad 4, which I would think takes up way more room than the Duke with its intake tubes. Has anyone improved the Quad 4 Fiero mounting design to eliminate the head gasket problem? Do people still put this engine in a Fiero instead of just going to a 3.4? It seems like it would be more work to get a Quad 4 and rewire it and fabricate the hoses and exhaust than to just go to a 3.4 and be done with it!<P>Good to see someone taking an interest in the 87 coupe!||||reg||10
Z||000005||Dave Gunsul||04-22-2001||12:03 PM||||Fiero Conversions Inc. in Canada sells a turbo for the 4cyl. Ph# (519)-972-4989<BR>IRM sells a dual exhuast. (301)-948-3301<BR>The Fiero shop sells a ported head and<BR>alum. flywheel. <A HREF="http://www.thefieroshop.com" TARGET=_blank>www.thefieroshop.com</A> <BR>And,although expensive,GM still sells the<BR>alum. super duty head.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000006||erikred||04-22-2001||04:24 PM||||Many performance parts are still available for this engine, you just need to be really resourceful to find them. A good start for finding info would be to search the archives for this forum, but be careful what you believe. I have found the truth often contradicts the advice given  when it comes to the subject of hopping up the 4 cyl. Do not believe the post that say it is not worth it! If you are going to rebuild your engine any way, it does not cost much more to go ahead and pump up the power.<P>The factory enternal componets are pretty whimpy though. The crankshaft is questionable and I would not even consider reusing the rods. Here are some ideas for strengthening it up.<P>-The internaly balanced crankshaft used on some 87 engines and all 88 up engines is supposed to be stronger than the the older design, although I cannot verify this at this time. If your Fiero does not have the internaly balanced crank, another possibility is to use a crankshaft from the old version of this engine. Before Pontiac started making it Chevy made it as a 153ci engine. It was used in Novas in the sixties. GM still manufactures it in 181ci form, it is sold under the Mercruiser name. These cranks are much stronger than the pontiac cranks and have even been used for racing. You will need to adjust your compression hieght and rod length to account for the different stroke of these cranks. There were a variety of stroke lengths available. <P>-Small journal 6" small block Chevy rods can be used.<P>-Forged pistons are available for this engine. Brand new they cost about $126 for a set of four.<P>-Camshafts are also readily available and inexpensive, if you go with a non roller one. So far I have not been able to locate a roller performance shaft, but one could be custom made.<P>I have done alot of research on building this engine and have found that there seems to be a many people looking for hop up info on it. I am thinking about building a web site all about this engine.<P>Erik-<P>||||reg||14
Z||000007||85Josh||04-22-2001||07:43 PM||||Erikred, where did you find the forged pistons?||||reg||1
Z||000008||baldlobo||04-22-2001||08:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by erikred:<B><BR>-The internaly balanced crankshaft used on some 87 engines and all 88 up engines is supposed to be stronger than the the older design, although I cannot verify this at this time. If your Fiero does not have the internaly balanced crank, another possibility is to use a crankshaft from the old version of this engine. Before Pontiac started making it Chevy made it as a 153ci engine. It was used in Novas in the sixties. GM still manufactures it in 181ci form, it is sold under the Mercruiser name. These cranks are much stronger than the pontiac cranks and have even been used for racing. You will need to adjust your compression hieght and rod length to account for the different stroke of these cranks. There were a variety of stroke lengths available.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE> <BR>First of all the Iron Duke was the last engine pontiac produced and has a stroke of 3" not anything else; the 153ci was made by hacking off two cylanders off of the chevy inlinesix.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by erikred:<B><BR>-Small journal 6" small block Chevy rods can be used.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>they use 6.054<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by erikred:<B><BR>-Forged pistons are available for this engine. Brand new they cost about $126 for a set of four.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I would like the manufactures name of the a formention Forged pistons.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||erikred||04-23-2001||12:35 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by baldlobo:<BR>[First of all the Iron Duke was the last engine pontiac produced and has a stroke of 3" not anything else; the 153ci was made by hacking off two cylanders off of the chevy inlinesix.<P>Yes, and pontiac took the Chevy 153, revised it and called theirs. If you do not believe me look in the "GM Performance Parts" catalog page 149 four cylinder performance head part #347056 "This cast-iron head is designed to be used on 151ci or 153ci four cylinder engines." Note that this head is also a non-cross flow design. There is also a recent post on this forum from someone who claims to already have a 153 crank in his Iron Duke. Look for the thread about Iron Duke crankshafts.<P> [QUOTE]Originally posted by erikred:<B><BR>-Small journal 6" small block Chevy rods can be used.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>they use 6.054<P>.054 inch can be made up for in the engine build or ignored.<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by erikred:<B><BR>-Forged pistons are available for this engine. Brand new they cost about $126 for a set of four.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I would like the manufactures name of the a formention Forged pistons.[/B][/QUOTE]<P>I think they were TRW, but I am not sure. Go to <A HREF="http://www.enginerebuilderssupply.com" TARGET=_blank>www.enginerebuilderssupply.com</A>  or call 1-800-334-5191 and ask them (they are not listed on the web site). I called them and $126 was the aproximate total for four forged pistons in stock compression.<P>Erik-<P>||||reg||9
Z||000010||erikred||04-23-2001||12:49 AM||||I do not have the latest catalog yet, but in my 97 catalog from PAW they list pins and forged pistons for $218. They also list two different performance cams for $59.95 ea.<P>Erik-||||reg||10
Z||000011||mrossum||04-23-2001||09:09 AM||||I have the 153 crank in my 151 block. put it in last night. I also have Crower 6 inch, not 6.054 inch, rods. with .030 over stock style hypereutectic pistons, the comp ratio is 10.57 : 1.||||reg||1
Z||000012||qwikgta||04-23-2001||03:29 PM||||WKDFIRO...Remember, to convert to a 3.4L you need to get a lot of stock V6 parts..ECM, harness, heads, intake ect.. then the donor motor, then mate them together.  Us 4cly guys have to start way behind the V6 guys who want to do this convert..  I am going to go the V8 4.5/4.9 Caddie way myself, I was offering a sugestion to someone who seemed intimadated with the V6/V8 swap.<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html<BR>http://vafiero.com||||reg||1
Z||000013||WKDFIRO||04-23-2001||04:53 PM||||I used to be a 4 cyl guy and it still has a place in my heart. I don't think that it would be too difficult to find a 3.4 long block find a trashed complete Fiero 2.8 (maybe because they just seem to be plentiful in California) and just put them together. <P>I would put it to you that it takes just as much prep to put in a Quad 4 as it does to put in a V8. Both need to be rewired to use the guauges and special consideration needs to be put into how it will be mounted, etc  in a Fiero. Whereas the 2.8/3.4 V6 is more standardized. You can go to another Fiero and see how it was done from the factory as opposed to finding a V8 to see how that particular owner did it. I understand where you are coming from Rob, I would go 3800 SC myself instead of V8, Im just looking at it from another point of view. My contention was that Quad 4 when they first were first being put into Fieros were having problems with the torque strut being bolted onto the aluminum head. Head gaskets blowing out became a common problem. I think its a good engine but never heard if this problem was taken care of.<BR>   Either path is a road to adventure, its just my view that the 2.8/3.4 V6 conversion from a Duke is the path more traveled.<P>Still, I would agree up to a point with what Erikred (welcome to the Forum) you will get a lot of people telling you its not worth it. It depends on what you realisticly expect out of the Duke. When I had my Duke I simply wanted it to run a little more efficiently. I thought the stock manifold was a tasteless joke and I would have probably ported the heads to increase flow. I wasn't expecting to beat the GTs I just wanted MY coupe to run a little quicker. Its up to the owner's budget of how much power they want.<BR>   If you are going to try to make the jump of making the Duke STRONGER than the V6 thats where you have to really consider if you want your Fiero to be that unique. Because it will cost.<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 04-23-2001).]||||reg||10
